    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220">

                <pb facs="0197" n="187" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3–1849: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-18T09:00:00+08:00">March 18, 1949—9
                            a. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:10 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>595. Ten-man peace committee has not functioned since Sun Fo resignation by which
                    time it had prepared first draft of peace formula. Reported Act[ing] President
                    will authorize Premier Ho Ying-chin select new committee which now depleted by
                    departure Sun Fo, Wu Tieh-cheng, Chang Chun<anchor n="25" xml:id="d220-f1"/> and
                    resignation Chung Tien-hsin.</p>
                <p>Peng Chao-hsien (C–C clique<anchor n="26" xml:id="d220-f2"/>) and Chung Tien-hsin
                    (Sun Fo adherent) resigned as peace delegates for forthcoming peace
                    negotiations, leaving Chang Chih-chung, Wu Chung-hsin and Shao Li-tze. Rumored
                    W. W. Yen, Chang Shih-chao and Chiang Yung (Shanghai peace delegates) are
                    favorites to succeed Huang and Chung.</p>
                <p>In telephone conversation from Peiping, Li Tsung-jen emissary Huang Chi-han
                    reportedly informed Li [that] Communists will name peace delegation, set time
                    and designate place for opening of peace negotiations before March 20.</p>
                <p>Sent Department 595, repeated Shanghai 272, Canton 169, Peiping 88.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d220-f1" n="25" xml:id="d220-f1-content">
                <p>Military and Political Affairs Director for Southwest China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d220-f2" n="26" xml:id="d220-f2-content">
                <p>Kuomintang group led by two brothers, Chen Li-fu and Chen Kuo-fu.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
